/*
# Bar

A labeled horizontal value bar: an optional caption, then a rounded track filled to
`value`. A **placeholder component** (composition.md §1a) — coherent and restyleable,
but not the final widget. For a themed, theme-tracking meter reach for `ProgressBar`
instead; `Bar` exists for demos and dashboards that want a caption and their own colour.

```declare
col: View [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 260,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ],
    Bar [ width = 260, label = "Storage", value = 72 ],
    Bar [ width = 260, label = "Memory",  value = 38, tint = #37E0C8 ]
    ]
```

## value
How full the track reads, **0–100 always** — a percentage, not your own units. This is
the difference from `ProgressBar`, which takes `min`/`max` and lets you pass raw counts.

## label
The caption to the left of the track. `""` drops the caption column entirely and the
track takes the full width.

## tint
The fill colour. **The one restyling knob** — `Bar` predates the theme tokens and
carries literal defaults, so it does not follow a theme the way library controls do.

## lead
The caption column's width, derived from whether there is a `label` at all. Set it to
align several bars whose captions differ in length.
*/

class Bar extends View [ height = 28,
    label: string = "",
    value: number = 0,
    tint:  Color  = #4C8DFF,
    lead:  number = { label == "" ? 0 : 96 },
    cap: Text [ y = 5, fontSize = 14, textColor = #8A9BA6, text = { classroot.label } ],
    rail: View [ x = { classroot.lead }, width = { parent.width - classroot.lead },
        height = { parent.height }, cornerRadius = 6, fill = #0A1119,
        bar: View [ height = { parent.height }, cornerRadius = 6, fill = { classroot.tint },
            width = { parent.width * classroot.value / 100 } ]
        ]
    ]
